          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "oupn7qx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Cultural and Educational Rights (Articles 29–30)"
    },
    {
      "id": "mrk7tkc",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Article</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Provision</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Article 29</p>",
            "<p>Protection of language, script and culture of minorities</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Article 30</p>",
            "<p>Right of minorities to establish and administer educational institutions</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "qaj2i4g",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "8. Right to Constitutional Remedies (Articles 32–35)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gbel439",
      "content": "Citizens can approach Supreme Court or High Court to enforce Fundamental Rights."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Article 32 was referred to as the 'Heart and Soul of the Constitution' by Dr. B.R. Ambedkar.",
        "Rights under Articles 20 and 21 cannot be suspended even during a National Emergency.",
        "High Courts issue writs under Article 226, whereas the Supreme Court issues writs under Article 32."
      ],
      "id": "r8kof0s"
    },
    {
      "id": "b6a74ol",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Writs"
    },
    {
      "id": "vxv8d2j",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Writ</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Purpose</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Habeas Corpus</p>",
            "<p>Produce a person illegally detained</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mandamus</p>",
            "<p>Order to public authority to perform duty</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Prohibition</p>",
            "<p>Prevent lower court from exceeding jurisdiction</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Certiorari</p>",
            "<p>Transfer case from lower court to higher court</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Quo Warranto</p>",
            "<p>Challenge legality of holding public office</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ykg3ba8",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "EXAM Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3gdymro",
      "content": "• Fundamental Rights are given in Part III (Articles 12–35)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9zhmt53",
      "content": "• Article 32 is called the heart and soul of the Constitution."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "z3icmyk",
      "content": "• Article 17 abolishes untouchability."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hu79h3b",
      "content": "• Right to Education added by 86th Amendment."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ia0qga9",
      "content": "• There are currently six Fundamental Rights."
    }
  ],
  "enriched": true
}